# Simple DB server tier — COMMIT DURABILITY
#
# =============================================================================
# THE DURABILITY CONTRACT
# =============================================================================
#
# Increment 1 of the server tier applied a committed transaction to the
# IN-MEMORY tables only: `txn_commit` never called `save()`, so a process that
# died one instruction after replying `OK applied=1` lost the write entirely.
# This module is the commit's durable half.  It adds NO storage engine and NO
# locking engine of its own: the durable write is `SdnDatabase.save()`, which
# is `atomic_write()` from `std.database.atomic` — `FileLock` (O_EXCL + pid
# liveness) around temp-file -> `rt_file_sync` -> `rt_file_rename`.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. What is guaranteed once COMMIT returns OK
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# When `durable_commit` returns `ok == true` and `persisted == true`, EVERY
# write of that transaction is in the database file on durable media, and a
# subsequent `SdnDatabase.load(path)` — in this process or in a brand new one,
# after a power cut — observes ALL of them.  The commit is all-or-nothing:
# there is no outcome in which some overlay entries are durable and others are
# not, because the durable step is a single whole-file rename.
#
# When `ok == false`, NOTHING of the transaction is durable and the in-memory
# store has been returned to its last durable state (see §4).
#
# EXPLICIT NON-GUARANTEES (do not read more into this than is written):
#   * The server does not rely on transient `SdnRow._version`: txn replay adds
#     the declared `_db_server_version` column and persists it with each row,
#     so optimistic conflicts remain meaningful after reopen.
#   * A field whose column is not in `SdnTable.columns` is NOT durable — it
#     lives in memory and is dropped by `to_sdn()`.  Callers must declare
#     their columns.  This is a store property, not a server-tier one.
#   * "Durable" here means the bytes reached the kernel and the file was
#     fsync'd before the rename.  It does not claim anything about a disk
#     controller that lies about its own write cache.
#   * Durability is per-COMMIT, not per-PUT.  A PUT inside an open transaction
#     is buffered in the session overlay and is not durable until COMMIT.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. What a reader sees mid-commit
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# In-process: the capsule serves one message at a time, so a peer session can
# only ever observe the store BETWEEN commits — never between the overlay
# apply and the persist.  (When the listener increment introduces real
# concurrency this stops being free; it is recorded as a listener-increment
# obligation, not as something already solved here.)
#
# Cross-process: a reader goes through `atomic_read`/`SdnDatabase.load`, which
# takes the SAME `FileLock` on the SAME path that the writer takes, and the
# visible swap is a `rename(2)`.  A reader therefore observes either the whole
# pre-commit file or the whole post-commit file.  It can never observe a torn
# mixture, for two independent reasons:
#   (a) the partially-written bytes only ever exist at `<path>.tmp`, a name no
#       reader opens; and
#   (b) the file body carries a `#sdn-crc32:` header, so even a torn file that
#       somehow reached `<path>` is REJECTED by `load()` (returns nil) rather
#       than parsed into a half-new state.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Crash between overlay-apply and persist
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The commit pipeline is:
#
#     P1 precheck   optimistic version check over the whole overlay
#     P2 undo       snapshot the pre-image of every key the overlay touches
#     P3 apply      overlay -> in-memory tables
#     P4 persist    SdnDatabase.save()  == FileLock + temp + fsync + rename
#     P5 ack        return OK to the client
#
# The COMMIT POINT is the rename inside P4.  Crash behaviour, per point:
#
#   | crash at            | on-disk state after restart | client saw |
#   |---------------------|-----------------------------|------------|
#   | during/after P1-P3  | OLD value everywhere        | nothing    |
#   | during P4, pre-rename| OLD value everywhere       | nothing    |
#   |                     | (a stray <path>.tmp may     |            |
#   |                     |  exist and is ignored)      |            |
#   | after P4, before P5 | NEW value everywhere        | nothing    |
#
# The last row is the honest one: a lost acknowledgement means the client
# cannot tell whether the commit landed.  This tier is AT-LEAST-ONCE at the
# connection level — a client that reconnects after a dropped connection MUST
# re-read to find out, and must not assume its retry is a no-op.  Making that
# idempotent needs a commit id in the protocol; it is NOT done here.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Recoverable persist failure (no crash)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# If `save()` returns false (lock not obtainable, disk full, read-only mount)
# the process is still alive and its memory would otherwise disagree with the
# disk.  The pre-image captured at P2 is replayed, so the in-memory store is
# put back to exactly its last durable state, and COMMIT answers
# `ERR code=durability`.  Memory and disk agree on the OLD value everywhere.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Why the WAL is deliberately NOT the commit point
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `std.database.wal` has entry types Insert/Update/Delete/Checkpoint and no
# transaction-boundary record.  A multi-write transaction appended entry by
# entry is therefore TORN by a crash in the middle of the append run: replay
# would resurrect a prefix of a transaction that never committed.  Using it as
# the commit point would need a Begin/Commit marker in `wal.spl`, which this
# lane does not own.  So the WAL keeps its existing role — it is checkpointed
# by `save()` — and whole-file atomic rename is the commit point.  Recorded as
# the reason, not as an oversight.

use std.database.core.{ SdnRow, SdnTable, SdnDatabase }
use std.database.atomic.{ file_write, file_exists }
use std.database.server.txn.{ TxnState, TxnWrite, CommitOutcome, txn_precheck, txn_apply }
use std.common.crypto.sha256.{ sha256_text }

# ============================================================================
# Fault-injection points
#
# These exist so the crash-point matrix is EXECUTABLE.  They are inert unless
# a caller explicitly asks for one; production callers use `durability_on()`.
# ============================================================================

val CRASH_NONE: text = ""
# Process dies after the overlay is applied in memory, before save() is called.
val CRASH_BEFORE_PERSIST: text = "before_persist"
# Process dies inside save(): the temp file exists, the rename never happened.
val CRASH_MID_PERSIST: text = "mid_persist"
# Process dies after the rename, before the client is acknowledged.
val CRASH_AFTER_PERSIST: text = "after_persist"
# save() returns false and the process keeps running (disk full / lock lost).
val CRASH_PERSIST_FAILS: text = "persist_fails"

struct DurabilityPolicy:
    enabled: bool
    crash_at: text

fn durability_on() -> DurabilityPolicy:
    DurabilityPolicy(enabled: true, crash_at: CRASH_NONE)

fn durability_off() -> DurabilityPolicy:
    DurabilityPolicy(enabled: false, crash_at: CRASH_NONE)

fn durability_crash_at(point: text) -> DurabilityPolicy:
    DurabilityPolicy(enabled: true, crash_at: point)

# ============================================================================
# Commit outcome, durability-aware
# ============================================================================

struct DurableOutcome:
    ok: bool            # true only when the client may be told OK
    crashed: bool       # a simulated crash fired; no acknowledgement exists
    persisted: bool     # the durable rename completed
    applied: i64
    conflict_key: text
    message: text

val COMMIT_TABLE: text = "_db_server_commits"
# Receipts are retained for the lifetime of the database file. There is no
# automatic pruning because deleting a receipt can make an old retry execute
# twice. At the hard cap, unseen commit IDs fail closed; existing IDs remain
# replayable. An operator must checkpoint/migrate receipts with an application-
# specific retry-age guarantee before admitting more IDs.
val MAX_COMMIT_RECEIPTS: i64 = 4096

fn _lower_hex_digest(value: text) -> bool:
    val bytes = value.bytes()
    if bytes.len() != 64:
        return false
    for byte in bytes:
        if not (byte >= 48 and byte <= 57) and not (byte >= 97 and byte <= 102):
            return false
    true

fn commit_receipt_capacity_available(store: SdnDatabase) -> bool:
    match store.get_table(COMMIT_TABLE):
        Some(table):
            val typed: SdnTable = table
            typed.rows.len() < MAX_COMMIT_RECEIPTS
        nil:
            true

fn committed_retry(store: SdnDatabase, commit_id: text, principal: text) -> i64:
    if commit_id == "":
        return -1
    match store.get_table(COMMIT_TABLE):
        Some(table):
            val typed: SdnTable = table
            if (not typed.columns.contains("id") or
                not typed.columns.contains("principal") or
                not typed.columns.contains("applied") or
                not typed.columns.contains("identity") or
                not typed.columns.contains("valid")):
                return -3
            match typed.get_row(commit_id):
                Some(row):
                    if row.get("valid") != Some("true"):
                        return -3
                    if (row.get("principal") ?? "") != principal:
                        return -2
                    val applied = row.get_i64("applied")
                    val identity = row.get("identity") ?? ""
                    if applied == nil or (applied ?? -1) < 0 or not _lower_hex_digest(identity):
                        return -3
                    applied ?? 0
                nil:
                    -1
        nil:
            -1

fn _identity_atom(value: text) -> text:
    val size = value.chars().len()
    "{size}:{value}"

fn transaction_identity(state: TxnState) -> text:
    var parts: [text] = []
    for write in state.writes:
        val typed: TxnWrite = write
        var keys: [text] = []
        for (key, _value) in typed.fields:
            keys.push(key)
        keys.sort()
        var fields: [text] = []
        for key in keys:
            fields.push(_identity_atom(key) + _identity_atom(typed.fields[key]))
        parts.push(
            _identity_atom(typed.table) + _identity_atom(typed.key) +
            _identity_atom(typed.kind) + _identity_atom("{typed.base_version}") +
            _identity_atom(fields.join(""))
        )
    val write_count = state.writes.len()
    # Persist only a fixed-size digest: commit receipts must not duplicate
    # application keys or values in the internal metadata table.
    sha256_text(_identity_atom("{write_count}") + parts.join(""))

fn commit_identity_matches(store: SdnDatabase, commit_id: text, state: TxnState) -> bool:
    match store.get_table(COMMIT_TABLE):
        Some(table):
            val typed: SdnTable = table
            match typed.get_row(commit_id):
                Some(row):
                    (row.get("identity") ?? "") == transaction_identity(state)
                nil:
                    false
        nil:
            false

fn record_commit(store: SdnDatabase, commit_id: text, applied: i64, state: TxnState, principal: text):
    if commit_id == "":
        return
    var table = match store.get_table(COMMIT_TABLE):
        Some(existing): existing
        nil: SdnTable.new(COMMIT_TABLE, ["id", "principal", "applied", "identity", "valid"])
    val row = SdnRow(fields: {}, _version: 0)
    row.set("id", commit_id)
    row.set("principal", principal)
    row.set("applied", "{applied}")
    row.set("identity", transaction_identity(state))
    row.set("valid", "true")
    table.add_row(row)
    store.set_table(COMMIT_TABLE, table)

fn durable_ok(applied: i64, persisted: bool, message: text) -> DurableOutcome:
    DurableOutcome(
        ok: true, crashed: false, persisted: persisted,
        applied: applied, conflict_key: "", message: message
    )

fn durable_err(key: text, message: text) -> DurableOutcome:
    DurableOutcome(
        ok: false, crashed: false, persisted: false,
        applied: 0, conflict_key: key, message: message
    )

fn durable_crash(point: text, persisted: bool, applied: i64) -> DurableOutcome:
    DurableOutcome(
        ok: false, crashed: true, persisted: persisted,
        applied: applied, conflict_key: "",
        message: "simulated crash at {point}; the client is never acknowledged"
    )

# ============================================================================
# Undo pre-images — how a recoverable persist failure gets memory back in
# agreement with disk
# ============================================================================

struct UndoEntry:
    table: text
    key: text
    existed: bool
    row: SdnRow

# Raw pre-image: unlike `store_read` this does NOT hide a soft-deleted row,
# because restoring a delete means restoring the row WITH its valid=false.
fn raw_row(store: SdnDatabase, table_name: text, key: text) -> SdnRow?:
    match store.get_table(table_name):
        Some(table):
            val typed: SdnTable = table
            typed.get_row(key)
        nil:
            nil

fn capture_undo(store: SdnDatabase, state: TxnState) -> [UndoEntry]:
    var undo: [UndoEntry] = []
    for write in state.writes:
        val typed: TxnWrite = write
        match raw_row(store, typed.table, typed.key):
            Some(row):
                val typed_row: SdnRow = row
                undo.push(UndoEntry(
                    table: typed.table, key: typed.key,
                    existed: true, row: typed_row.copy()
                ))
            nil:
                undo.push(UndoEntry(
                    table: typed.table, key: typed.key,
                    existed: false, row: SdnRow(fields: {}, _version: 0)
                ))
    undo

# Drop one row from a table.  `SdnTable` has no remove, and `core.spl` is not
# this lane's to change, so the table is rebuilt from its public surface.
fn drop_row(store: SdnDatabase, table_name: text, key: text):
    match store.get_table(table_name):
        Some(table):
            val typed: SdnTable = table
            var rebuilt = SdnTable.new(typed.name, typed.columns)
            for row in typed.rows:
                val typed_row: SdnRow = row
                if typed_row.get("id") != Some(key):
                    rebuilt.add_row(typed_row)
            store.set_table(table_name, rebuilt)
        nil:
            pass_do_nothing("table vanished; nothing to drop")

fn restore_undo(store: SdnDatabase, undo: [UndoEntry]):
    for entry in undo:
        val typed: UndoEntry = entry
        if typed.existed:
            # Extract -> mutate -> write back; a mutating call through
            # store.tables[name].update_row(...) loses the write on the
            # interpreter.
            match store.get_table_mut(typed.table):
                Some(table_value):
                    var table: SdnTable = table_value
                    table.update_row(typed.key, typed.row)
                    store.set_table(typed.table, table)
                nil:
                    pass_do_nothing("table vanished; nothing to restore")
        else:
            drop_row(store, typed.table, typed.key)

# ============================================================================
# The mid-persist crash model
# ============================================================================

# `atomic_write` writes `<path>.tmp`, fsyncs it, then renames it over `<path>`.
# A crash between those two steps leaves exactly this: a torn temp file and an
# untouched main file.  Reproducing it byte-for-byte is what makes the
# "reader never sees a torn mixture" claim testable rather than asserted.
fn write_torn_temp(path: text) -> bool:
    if path == "":
        return false
    file_write(path + ".tmp", "#sdn-crc32:0\nusers |id, name, valid|\n    u1, TRUNCA")

fn temp_path_exists(path: text) -> bool:
    file_exists(path + ".tmp")

# ============================================================================
# The durable commit
# ============================================================================

# Mutates `store` in place; callers use the extract -> mutate -> write back
# pattern (`var s = self.store; durable_commit(s, ...); self.store = s`).
fn durable_commit_identified(
    store: SdnDatabase, state: TxnState, policy: DurabilityPolicy, commit_id: text
) -> DurableOutcome:
    durable_commit_for_principal(store, state, policy, commit_id, "")

fn durable_commit_for_principal(
    store: SdnDatabase, state: TxnState, policy: DurabilityPolicy,
    commit_id: text, principal: text
) -> DurableOutcome:
    val retry_applied = committed_retry(store, commit_id, principal)
    if retry_applied == -2:
        return durable_err(commit_id, "commit id belongs to a different principal")
    if retry_applied == -3:
        return durable_err(commit_id, "commit receipt schema is invalid")
    if retry_applied >= 0:
        if state.open and not commit_identity_matches(store, commit_id, state):
            return durable_err(commit_id, "commit id already belongs to a different transaction")
        return durable_ok(retry_applied, store.path != "", "idempotent retry")
    if not state.open:
        return durable_err("", "no open transaction")
    if commit_id != "" and not commit_receipt_capacity_available(store):
        return durable_err(commit_id, "commit receipt capacity {MAX_COMMIT_RECEIPTS} reached")

    # ---- P1 precheck ----------------------------------------------------
    val pre: CommitOutcome = txn_precheck(store, state)
    if not pre.ok:
        return durable_err(pre.conflict_key, pre.message)

    # ---- P2 undo pre-images ---------------------------------------------
    val undo = capture_undo(store, state)

    # ---- P3 apply to memory ---------------------------------------------
    val applied_outcome: CommitOutcome = txn_apply(store, state)
    if not applied_outcome.ok:
        restore_undo(store, undo)
        return durable_err(applied_outcome.conflict_key, applied_outcome.message)
    val applied = applied_outcome.applied
    record_commit(store, commit_id, applied, state, principal)

    # A store with no path is an explicit in-memory model.  Say so; never
    # report it as durable.
    val durable_target = policy.enabled and store.path != ""
    if not durable_target:
        return durable_ok(applied, false, "in-memory only: durability disabled or store has no path")

    if policy.crash_at == CRASH_BEFORE_PERSIST:
        return durable_crash(CRASH_BEFORE_PERSIST, false, applied)

    if policy.crash_at == CRASH_MID_PERSIST:
        write_torn_temp(store.path)
        return durable_crash(CRASH_MID_PERSIST, false, applied)

    # ---- P4 persist — the commit point ----------------------------------
    var persisted = true
    if policy.crash_at == CRASH_PERSIST_FAILS:
        persisted = false
    else:
        persisted = store.save()

    if not persisted:
        restore_undo(store, undo)
        if commit_id != "":
            drop_row(store, COMMIT_TABLE, commit_id)
        return durable_err(
            "",
            "commit not persisted; in-memory store rolled back to its last durable state"
        )

    if policy.crash_at == CRASH_AFTER_PERSIST:
        return durable_crash(CRASH_AFTER_PERSIST, true, applied)

    # ---- P5 ack ----------------------------------------------------------
    durable_ok(applied, true, "")

fn durable_commit(store: SdnDatabase, state: TxnState, policy: DurabilityPolicy) -> DurableOutcome:
    durable_commit_identified(store, state, policy, "")

# ============================================================================
# Durable-state oracle
#
# Reads a value straight off disk with a FRESH load, bypassing every in-memory
# structure the server owns.  Crash-point assertions must use this: asking the
# server what it thinks it stored proves nothing about durability.
# ============================================================================

# nil = the key is absent (or soft-deleted, or the file is unreadable).
fn durable_value(path: text, table_name: text, key: text, column: text) -> text?:
    match SdnDatabase.load(path):
        Some(loaded):
            val db: SdnDatabase = loaded
            match db.get_table(table_name):
                Some(table):
                    val typed: SdnTable = table
                    match typed.get_row(key):
                        Some(row):
                            val typed_row: SdnRow = row
                            if typed_row.get("valid") == Some("false"):
                                return nil
                            typed_row.get(column)
                        nil:
                            nil
                nil:
                    nil
        nil:
            nil

# true when the file on disk parses at all.  A torn file fails its crc32
# header and loads as nil — that is the fail-closed half of the "never a torn
# mixture" claim.
fn durable_file_loads(path: text) -> bool:
    SdnDatabase.load(path).?
